From c58cff89be19c181f20053fc2b9fc0d19f3a619f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:40:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] v3.1.7: walker coverage, the Killswitch witness, and the UI
 that reports it

Firewall table walker, second pass: 33 -> 45 of the 59 catalog rows.

  C6, the VPN-client Killswitch, needed a FOURTH INPUT. It is realised solely
  as `ip rule ... prohibit` at pref 9100+code and never touches iptables, so no
  walk of the tables could see it and a Killswitch that failed to install was
  invisible - the exact failure the feature exists to prevent. The walker now
  reads the routing policy database alongside iptables-save, ip addr and ipset
  test, and a PROHIBIT predicate asserts that a rule refusing that fwmark
  exists. Still read-only. Rows are driven from the SAVED policy-routing list,
  for F1's reason: a witness derived from the installed state can only agree
  with itself. Only an ENABLED client's Killswitch counts, as v3.1.5 settled,
  so a disabled client emits no row rather than a red one.

  Eleven more rows: B3 B5 C2 C4 D5 E3 E6 E7 F5 H1 H6. Three are worth more than
  their catalog line. B5 is keyed on UPnP's DAEMON nvram key and asserts its
  RULES key, so the desync class that has been seen in both directions on metal
  is now a red witness naming the missing jump. D5 witnesses that a
  Gatekeeper-BLOCKED device can still reach the HTTPS admin port - the escape
  hatch - and its negative is the rule that would lock an admin out of their own
  router. F5 is the intercept loop-breaker: the host a service is redirected TO
  must reach the real service itself, or its own upstream query comes back at it
  and the service fails for everyone while F4 still reads green.

  Not built, with reasons: C8 would need a /proc/blog reader and that interface
  has a kernel-panic history here; H4 (IPv6 over the generated rows) is deferred
  because guest/SDN IPv6 is not proven and a row that goes red on a working box
  is worse than no row.

Bare links no longer render browser-blue. One rule in the injected theme reaches
framed stock pages and native pages alike. It is wrapped in :where() so it has
zero specificity: the block is injected at </head>, after a page's own <style>,
so on equal specificity it would have silently restyled the five pages that were
already correct. It is a floor, not an override.

The Security Posture card refreshes itself on NTP sync. It was painted once from
a server-rendered nvram snapshot, so on a booting router it froze at load - and
the Rule Status row was worse, because the walker stamps time(NULL) and a
pre-sync report is dated from the boot epoch. The watcher arms only when the
clock is already unset at render, so an ordinary page load schedules nothing at
all, and it stops on the transition.

The firewall's red count now rides the nav rail on every page, and shows nothing
at zero. One poll per session, not per page. The BETA tag is on every header
rather than only the dashboard's, and --amber came with it - the shell had never
declared that token, so the tag rendered in the wrong colour once it moved.

Rule Status reads properly at 1080p: the page's reading column was 1120px, set
when every tab was a form, and the five-column table had nowhere to go. Columns
are fixed proportions now, and overflow-wrap:anywhere replaces
word-break:break-all, which had been splitting words that had a space to wrap at.

Top talkers counts conversations, not connections. Every open admin page holds
several parallel connections to the router, so looking at the traffic page
manufactured the traffic it then reported - and since each connection took a slot
of its own, a browser on the dashboard could fill the table and evict everything
real. Flows now fold by the tuple the panel claims to group by, and the router
itself is excluded, which is the rule the per-network view has always used.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
